Magnesium is involved in converting food into energy and in the normal function of the parathyroid glands, which are important for bone health. Good food sources include spinach, nuts and wholemeal bread. In the UK, the recommended daily intake is 300 mg for adult men and 270 mg for adult women aged 19‑64. Taking more than 400 mg at once may cause diarrhoea. The British National Formulary notes that oral magnesium glycerophosphate is an unlicensed preparation used in the NHS and is available only through special‑order manufacturers.

